c sum approximation, S(4)=4.7596>4.\n\nThis indicates a problem with the model. Let\'s think again.\n\nPerhaps the correct model is that after each minute, the ant\'s position is scaled by (L(t)/L(t-1)), and the ant\'s movement up to that point is scaled accordingly.\n\nLet me explain. Suppose at time t, the band is length L(t) =4 +t. The ant moves 1 m relative to the band. So, if we denote P(t) as the ant\'s position relative to the left end, then:\n\nP(t) = sum_{k=1}^t 1 * (L(k)/L(k-1))\n\nBecause each movement of 1 m relative to L(k) band is scaled by L(k)/L(k-1) when converting to the next length.\n\nWait, actually, when the band is stretched from L(k-1) to L(k), the position P(k-1) scales to P(k) = P(k-1) * (L(k)/L(k-1)).\n\nBut since each step the ant moves 1 m relative to L(k), then P(k) = P(k-1) + 1 * (L(k)/L(k-1)).\n\nBecause moving 1 m relative to the band means that if the band is longer, the ant moves further.\n\nTherefore, the cumulative position is:\n\nP(t) = sum_{k=1}^t (L(k)/L(k-1))\n\nBut L(k) =4 +k, so:\n\nP(t) = sum_{k=1}^t (4 +k)/(4 +k -1) = sum_{k=1}^t (4 +k)/(3 +k)\n\nThis is exactly the sum we considered before.\n\nTherefore, the ant\'s position relative to the left end after t minutes is P(t) = sum_{k=1}^t (4 +k)/(3 +k). We need to find the minimal t such that P(t) >=4.\n\nBut the problem is that P(t) is relative to the left end, but the right end has moved as well. The right end is at L(t) =4 +t. So, the ant reaches the right end when P(t) >=4 +t.\n\nWait, no. Wait, if the band is stretched to length4 +t, then the left end is at0, and the right end is at4 +t. The ant\'s position is P(t) relative to the left end. So, the ant reaches the right end when P(t) >=4 +t.\n\nWait, but no, if the right end is moving to4 +t, then to reach the right end, the ant\'s position must be equal to the right end\'s position.\n\nRight end at time t is at4 +t. The ant\'s position at time t is P(t). But each movement of the ant is relative to the band\'s length at